# Working with Figma

The live file is the spec. Screenshots, Linear images, and layer lists are
not. Follow `use-figma` to open it. This rule is how to interpret what you
read.

## Read the thing, not the label

A node's `name` is a label. It is not the copy, the type role, the
component, or proof that two nodes are the same.

Read the actual characters, fills, strokes, text styles, bound variables,
font size/weight, and unrotated width/height. Two layers named "Jeff"
can be different styles. A "Description" layer can hold different sentences.

When proposing a change, point at a place a human can find: page, nearby
copy, and a file URL with `node-id`. Ids alone are not enough.

## Names in code

Figma names stay in Figma. JSX, files, and CSS names follow this repo
(`Home`, `root_layout`, `className` utilities). Treat canvas names as
placeholders.

## Layout

Figma frames are a starting point, not the HTML. Absolute children, extra
wrappers, and auto-layout that fights the document are issues — propose a
structure that works in the page and offer the matching Figma edit. Do not
silently diverge, and do not copy a bad frame tree into JSX.

Transformed frames: use unrotated size and spacing. Bounding-box `x`/`y`
after rotation is not misalignment.

## Writes

Propose the concrete edit (what, why, which node). Wait for a yes on that
edit. Then write it. A group of findings is not approval.
